"""Generates the Phase 8 MCQ expansion drafts (XQ-01..XQ-11, 66 questions) as markdown in the same MCQ format the existing draft validator and audit use.
Every question carries a **Trace** line naming the registered source(s) and the statement it rests on. All files are unreviewed drafts, never imported."""
import os
OUT=os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))
L="ABCD"
# each question: (tag, stem, correct, why_correct, [(distractor, why_not)x3], trace_ids, trace_text)
def Q(tag,stem,correct,why,ds,ids,trace): return dict(tag=tag,stem=stem,correct=correct,why=why,ds=ds,ids=ids,trace=trace)
SETS=[]
def S(sid,slug,title,skills,sources,refs,qs): SETS.append(dict(id=sid,slug=slug,title=title,skills=skills,sources=sources,refs=refs,qs=qs))
CAUTI="N-CDC-CAUTI-2009"; BSI="S-CDC-BSI-2011"; ICS="N-ICS-TRANSFER-2026"; BTS="N-BTS-PCS-2023"; HOD="N-HODGSON-2014"; AHS="N-AHS-CRRT-ALARM-2025"
S("XQ-01","urinary-catheter","Urinary catheter care (CAUTI prevention)",["SK-12"],[CAUTI],[("CDC/HICPAC Guideline for Prevention of Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infections (2009)","https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/media/pdfs/Guideline-CAUTI-H.pdf")],[
 Q("judgment","A ventilated patient has had an indwelling urinary catheter for three days to measure hourly output. He is now off sedation, stable and mobilising with physiotherapy, with no retention. What is the best nursing contribution on the morning round?",
   "Ask the prescriber whether the catheter is still needed.","The CDC guideline advises inserting catheters only for appropriate indications and leaving them only as long as needed; the nurse prompts the daily review and the prescriber decides.",
   [("Leave the catheter in until discharge from the unit, because removing it would increase the nursing workload on the ward.","Convenience is not an indication, and prolonged catheter days raise infection risk."),("Remove the catheter yourself without an order, because the patient is now mobilising and looks well.","Removal needs an order or an approved nurse-led protocol; the nurse's role is to raise the question."),("Change the catheter and drainage bag for a new set to reduce the infection risk on day three.","Routine fixed-interval changes are not recommended; changes follow clinical indications.")],[CAUTI],"Appropriate indications, leave only as long as needed (IB); no routine fixed-interval changes (II)."),
 Q("application","You help a catheterised patient from bed to a chair. The drainage bag is hanging on the arm of the chair, level with his abdomen. What should you do?",
   "Hang the bag below bladder level, off the floor, with the tubing free of kinks, before he sits.","The guideline says to keep the collecting bag below the level of the bladder at all times, not resting on the floor, and to keep the tubing free from kinks.",
   [("Leave the bag on the chair arm so urine can drain by gravity into the bag while he is seated.","A bag at bladder level or higher allows urine to flow back toward the bladder."),("Rest the bag on the floor beside the chair so it hangs well below the bladder and cannot tip.","The guideline also says the bag must not rest on the floor, because that risks contamination."),("Clamp the tubing for the time he is sitting so no urine reaches the bag and nothing can spill.","Clamping obstructs flow, and unobstructed urine flow is a recommended practice.")],[CAUTI],"Keep bag below bladder, not on floor; keep catheter and tubing free from kinking (IB)."),
 Q("safety","During a turn, the junction between a catheter and its drainage tube separates and the tube end touches the bed sheet. What is the best action?",
   "Replace the catheter and collecting system aseptically, using sterile equipment, and report the breach.","The guideline says that if aseptic technique breaks or the system disconnects or leaks, the catheter and collecting system should be replaced using aseptic technique and sterile equipment.",
   [("Wipe the tube end with an alcohol swab, reconnect it and carry on, because the interruption was only brief.","Reconnecting a contaminated closed system breaks the closed-drainage principle."),("Flush the tube end with antiseptic solution and reconnect, which should make the system safe again.","Antiseptic instillation is not a recommended way to restore a broken closed system."),("Leave the junction open to drain into a clean container until the doctor arrives to review it.","An open system increases infection risk and does not fix the breach.")],[CAUTI],"If breaks in aseptic technique, disconnection or leakage occur, replace catheter and collecting system aseptically (IB)."),
 Q("scope","A colleague suggests inserting a catheter in a patient whose skin is intact but who is incontinent overnight, 'to save time on the night shift'. What is the best response?",
   "Explain that the guideline advises against catheters for managing incontinence, and discuss alternatives with the team.","The guideline says to avoid urinary catheters for managing incontinence, and lists external catheters as an option in suitable patients.",
   [("Agree, since a catheter is the safest way to keep a bed dry and protects the skin from moisture.","Convenience is not an appropriate indication, and the catheter itself adds infection risk."),("Agree, provided the catheter is changed every few days so any infection risk stays low.","Routine fixed-interval changes are not recommended and do not make the indication appropriate."),("Insert it only after asking the family for consent, since consent makes the use appropriate.","Consent does not create an indication; the use still has to meet the guideline's appropriate indications.")],[CAUTI],"Avoid urinary catheters for management of incontinence (IB); consider external catheters in cooperative male patients (II)."),
 Q("judgment","A nurse plans to clean around the urethral meatus of a catheterised patient with an antiseptic solution every shift to prevent infection. What should you advise?",
   "Routine hygiene such as cleansing the meatal surface during daily bathing is appropriate.","The guideline says not to clean the periurethral area with antiseptics to prevent CAUTI while the catheter is in place, and that routine hygiene is appropriate.",
   [("Continue each shift, because antiseptic cleaning around the meatus is the main way to prevent infection.","This is the practice the guideline advises against."),("Increase to every few hours, because more frequent antiseptic cleaning should lower the infection risk further.","More frequent antiseptic cleaning is not recommended either."),("Stop all cleaning of the area so the catheter site is never disturbed during the admission.","Routine hygiene during daily bathing is still appropriate.")],[CAUTI],"Do not clean periurethral area with antiseptics to prevent CAUTI while catheter in place; routine hygiene appropriate (IB)."),
 Q("application","On a busy shift a nurse empties four patients' catheter bags into one measuring jug, rinsing it only at the end. What is the issue?",
   "Each bag should be emptied into a separate, clean container, avoiding splashing and contact between the spigot and the container.","The guideline says to empty the bag regularly using a separate, clean collecting container for each patient and to prevent contact of the drainage spigot with the container.",
   [("There is no issue, because the jug is rinsed afterwards and all four patients are on the same unit.","A shared container can carry organisms between patients; rinsing at the end is not enough."),("The only issue is the time spent, so empty the bags together and record one combined volume for all four.","Combined volumes also lose each patient's accurate output record."),("The jug is acceptable if the spigots touch the rim, since the rim is cleaned between patients.","Spigot contact with the container is itself the thing to avoid.")],[CAUTI],"Empty bag using a separate clean container for each patient; avoid splashing and spigot contact (IB)."),
])
S("XQ-02","peripheral-iv","Peripheral IV care (CDC 2011)",["SK-03"],[BSI],[("CDC/HICPAC Guidelines for the Prevention of Intravascular Catheter-Related Infections (2011; last update October 2017)","https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/media/pdfs/Guideline-BSI-H.pdf")],[
 Q("application","A patient has a peripheral catheter covered by an opaque gauze dressing and has no complaints or signs of infection. How should you assess the site on your daily check?",
   "Palpate through the dressing for tenderness and leave the gauze in place unless there are signs of infection.","CDC advises daily evaluation by palpation through the dressing, and says gauze and opaque dressings should not be removed if there are no clinical signs of infection.",
   [("Remove the dressing every shift to inspect the skin, then apply a fresh one to keep the site clean.","Needless removal is not advised when there are no signs of infection."),("Skip the check until the patient complains of pain, because tenderness is the only reliable sign.","The guideline asks for a daily evaluation, not waiting for symptoms."),("Inspect only the edge of the gauze and the tubing, since the skin under it cannot be assessed.","Palpation through the dressing is the recommended way to assess tenderness.")],[BSI],"Evaluate site daily by palpation through dressing; do not remove gauze/opaque dressing without signs of infection (II)."),
 Q("judgment","The skin over a patient's peripheral catheter is red and warm, and a tender cord can be felt along the vein. What is the right response?",
   "Stop the infusion, remove or arrange removal of the catheter per policy, and report the findings.","CDC advises removing a peripheral catheter if the patient develops phlebitis (warmth, tenderness, erythema or a palpable venous cord), infection or a malfunctioning catheter.",
   [("Flush the catheter gently and continue the infusion, then recheck the site at the next routine round.","Continuing to use a catheter with signs of phlebitis risks harm and infection."),("Apply a warm compress over the dressing and keep the catheter, because the symptoms may settle.","Comfort measures do not replace removal when phlebitis is present."),("Cover the area with a second dressing to protect the vein and record the finding in the notes.","Covering the site does not address the problem and delays removal.")],[BSI],"Remove peripheral venous catheters if phlebitis, infection or malfunction (IB)."),
 Q("safety","An adult's peripheral cannula was placed in a foot vein during an emergency. What should the nurse do once the emergency has settled?",
   "Ask for it to be replaced with an upper-extremity site as soon as possible.","CDC advises using an upper-extremity site in adults and replacing a catheter placed in a lower extremity as soon as possible.",
   [("Leave it until the routine replacement time, since the foot site works and the patient has no pain.","The guideline says to replace it as soon as possible, not at the routine time."),("Keep it because lower-limb veins are larger and carry a lower risk of infection in adults.","This reverses the guideline's advice for adults."),("Cover it with a bandage and elevate the leg so the site is protected from movement.","Protection does not address the preferred site recommendation.")],[BSI],"In adults use upper-extremity site; replace lower-extremity catheter as soon as possible (II)."),
 Q("application","After applying skin antiseptic to a patient's forearm, you want to feel for the vein once more before inserting a peripheral cannula. What should you do?",
   "Not palpate the site again unless aseptic technique is maintained, for example by using a sterile glove.","CDC says palpation of the insertion site should not be performed after the application of antiseptic unless aseptic technique is maintained.",
   [("Palpate with a clean bare finger, because the antiseptic has already killed the organisms on the skin.","Touching the prepared site contaminates it, which is why the guideline restricts re-palpation."),("Palpate with a gloved hand only after the antiseptic is fully dry, then insert straight away.","Dryness does not change the rule; aseptic technique has to be maintained."),("Apply more antiseptic after palpating and insert before it dries to save time on the procedure.","Re-applying antiseptic does not make unprotected palpation acceptable.")],[BSI],"Palpation after antiseptic only if aseptic technique maintained (IB hand hygiene and aseptic technique)."),
 Q("judgment","A patient needs about ten days of intravenous therapy and has poor peripheral veins. What can the nurse sensibly raise with the prescriber?",
   "Whether a midline catheter or a PICC would suit better than repeated short peripheral catheters.","CDC advises a midline catheter or PICC instead of a short peripheral catheter when therapy will likely exceed six days.",
   [("Whether to keep resiting short peripheral catheters every day, because that avoids any catheter-related infection.","Frequent resiting is not what the guideline recommends for longer therapy."),("Whether the same peripheral cannula can stay for the full ten days if the dressing is kept dry.","The guideline suggests peripheral catheters need not stay beyond the usual replacement interval."),("Whether a steel needle can be used, because it is quick to place and easy to remove.","Steel needles are not recommended where tissue necrosis could follow extravasation.")],[BSI],"Midline or PICC instead of short peripheral catheter when IV therapy likely to exceed six days (II)."),
 Q("scope","A colleague proposes a steel winged needle for a drug that can cause tissue necrosis if it leaks out of the vein. What is the best response?",
   "Avoid it and ask for a suitable catheter.","CDC advises avoiding steel needles for fluids and medication that might cause tissue necrosis if extravasation occurs (Category IA).",
   [("Accept it, because a steel needle is easier to remove if the drug starts to leak into the tissue.","Rigid needles are more likely to damage the vein wall and extravasate."),("Accept it if it is secured firmly with tape and checked every few minutes by two staff.","Close checking does not make a steel needle acceptable here."),("Accept it for one dose only, since a single short use should carry little risk of harm.","The advice is not limited by the number of doses.")],[BSI],"Avoid steel needles for fluids/medication that might cause tissue necrosis if extravasation occurs (IA)."),
])
S("XQ-03","central-line-care","Central venous catheter care (CDC 2011)",["SK-02"],[BSI],[("CDC/HICPAC Guidelines for the Prevention of Intravascular Catheter-Related Infections (2011; last update October 2017)","https://www.cdc.gov/infection-control/media/pdfs/Guideline-BSI-H.pdf")],[
 Q("application","You find the transparent dressing over a patient's central line lifting at one edge after he has been sweating. What is the right action?",
   "Replace the dressing using the unit's sterile technique now.","CDC advises replacing a catheter site dressing that becomes damp, loosened or visibly soiled (Category IB).",
   [("Press the edge back down and review it at the next scheduled dressing change.","A loosened dressing no longer protects the site."),("Add a second dressing over the first one to keep the edge down until the next change.","Layering over a compromised dressing does not restore a sterile cover."),("Tape the lifted edge with clean tape so the original dressing stays in place for its full interval.","Tape does not make a loosened dressing sterile again.")],[BSI],"Replace dressing if damp, loosened or visibly soiled (IB)."),
 Q("safety","A colleague is about to give an intermittent injection through the needleless connector of a central line. What must happen first?",
   "The connector should be disinfected with an appropriate antiseptic before the access.","CDC (Category IA) and the SHEA 2022 essential practices call for disinfecting hubs, needleless connectors and ports before every access.",
   [("Nothing extra is needed because the connector was disinfected at the start of the shift.","Disinfection is required before every access."),("Wipe the connector only if it looks visibly dirty, since clean connectors carry little risk.","The practice applies every time, not only when the connector looks dirty."),("Flush the connector with sterile water first, which removes any organisms from the outside.","Flushing does not disinfect the outside surface of the connector.")],[BSI,"S-SHEA-CLABSI-2022"],"Disinfect hubs, connectors and ports before every access (IA)."),
 Q("judgment","A nurse wants to put antibiotic ointment on a central line insertion site 'to be extra safe' for a patient who is not on dialysis. What should you tell them?",
   "CDC advises against topical antibiotic ointments or creams at insertion sites, except for dialysis catheters.","CDC says not to use topical antibiotic ointment or creams on insertion sites except for dialysis catheters, because they may promote fungal infections and resistance.",
   [("It is a good idea, because ointment adds a barrier that lowers the chance of bacteria reaching the skin.","This is the practice the guideline advises against."),("It is acceptable if a thin film is used and the site is cleaned at every dressing change.","The thickness of the film does not change the recommendation."),("It is acceptable for patients with fever, because fever suggests the site may be colonised.","Fever calls for assessment and escalation, not ointment.")],[BSI],"Do not use topical antibiotic ointment on insertion sites except dialysis catheters (IB)."),
 Q("application","A patient with a central line asks to shower. Which advice fits the CDC guideline?",
   "Showering can be allowed if the catheter and connecting device are protected with an impermeable cover, but the site must not be submerged.","CDC says not to submerge the catheter or site in water, and that showering is permitted if precautions reduce the chance of introducing organisms, such as an impermeable cover.",
   [("No water contact at all is allowed for as long as the line is in place, so offer only a bed bath.","The guideline allows showering with precautions."),("Showering is fine without cover if the dressing is a transparent one and the shower is short.","The catheter and connecting device still need protection."),("A bath is fine as long as the line site stays above the water level for most of the time.","Submerging the catheter or site is not advised.")],[BSI],"Do not submerge catheter/site; showering permitted with protection (IB)."),
 Q("monitoring","A short-term central line has a transparent dressing labelled eight days ago and still looks intact. How should you act?",
   "Recognise that the usual interval for transparent dressings on short-term lines is at least weekly and follow your unit's interval.","CDC advises replacing transparent dressings on short-term CVC sites at least every 7 days (Category IB), with exceptions in children; local policy sets the exact interval.",
   [("Leave it, because an intact dressing never needs to be changed until it becomes damp or soiled.","An interval applies even when the dressing looks intact."),("Change it only if the patient develops a fever, because fever is the signal to look at the site.","Waiting for fever delays a scheduled change."),("Replace only the label and the tape so the dressing stays in place and the date looks current.","Relabelling does not renew a dressing that has passed its interval.")],[BSI],"Transparent dressings on short-term CVC sites replaced at least every 7 days (IB); follow unit interval."),
 Q("scope","On the ward round it looks as if a patient's central line has not been used for two days and he is well. What is the best nursing contribution?",
   "Ask the team whether the line is still needed.","CDC and SHEA both emphasise reviewing line necessity and removing lines that are no longer needed; removal itself is a prescriber decision.",
   [("Leave it in place as a precaution, because a line that is already in carries no extra risk.","Every line day carries infection risk."),("Remove it yourself immediately, since you are the person who looks after the line every day.","Removal needs an order or an approved protocol."),("Change the dressing and flush the line, because this shows the line is working and may stay.","Maintenance does not answer whether the line is needed.")],[BSI,"S-SHEA-CLABSI-2022"],"Remove nonessential lines; review daily necessity."),
])
S("XQ-04","patient-transport","Intrahospital and interhospital transfer (ICS 2026)",["SK-20"],[ICS],[("Intensive Care Society. Guidance on the Transfer of the Critically Ill Adult, 5th edition (22 April 2026)","https://ics.ac.uk/asset/75861CB3%2DB6B4%2D4E78%2D80F22B290BC05015/")],[
 Q("judgment","A patient with low blood pressure and rising oxygen needs is booked for a CT scan and the transport assistant has arrived. What does the transfer guidance support?",
   "Resuscitate and stabilise the patient first.","ICS recommendation 58 says patients should be appropriately resuscitated and stabilised before transfer to reduce the physiological disturbance of movement.",
   [("Leave immediately, since the scan will give the team the diagnosis that guides all further treatment.","Time-critical transfers exist, but stabilisation is the default expectation."),("Send the patient with the transport assistant and ask the radiographer to watch the monitor in the scanner room.","Transfers need trained escorts and continuous monitoring."),("Wait until the patient's blood pressure is back in the normal range, even if that delays an urgent life-saving scan.","The guidance does not ask for normal values; it asks for appropriate stabilisation and warns against delaying time-critical transfers.")],[ICS],"Rec 58: resuscitate and stabilise before transfer."),
 Q("application","As you prepare to leave, the monitor leads and syringe-driver cables are tangled across the trolley and the screens face away from you. What should you do?",
   "Tie the monitoring cables together and position the monitors, ventilator display and syringe drivers where the escorts can see them.","ICS recommendations 74-75 say monitors, ventilator displays and syringe drivers should be visible to accompanying staff and monitoring cables tied together to form an umbilicus.",
   [("Leave the cables loose so they can move freely when the trolley goes over bumps in the corridor.","Loose cables are a safety risk and are not advised."),("Turn the monitor away and rely on the alarms, because alarms alert staff to any change.","Staff are expected to see the displays during transfer."),("Disconnect the syringe drivers and carry them in the bag to keep the trolley uncluttered.","Interrupting infusions without a plan is unsafe.")],[ICS],"Rec 74-75: displays visible; cables tied together (umbilicus)."),
 Q("monitoring","A scan and return is expected to take about forty minutes, and the vasoactive syringe is nearly empty. What does the guidance support?",
   "Making sure there is enough medication for at least twice the planned duration of the transfer.","ICS recommendation 65 says sufficient bolus and infusion medications should be available for at least twice the duration of the planned transfer.",
   [("Starting the transfer and replacing the syringe at the scanner, because a change takes only a moment.","Changes during transfer are riskier than planning ahead."),("Asking the transport assistant to fetch another syringe if the infusion runs out while the patient is in the scanner.","A transport assistant cannot prepare or give critical infusions."),("Stopping the infusion for the scan, since the patient will be lying still and should not need it.","Stopping a critical infusion without a plan risks deterioration.")],[ICS],"Rec 65: infusions for at least twice the planned duration."),
 Q("scope","The team plans to move a critically unwell, ventilated patient with one nurse as the only escort. What should you raise?",
   "Higher-risk patients need two appropriately trained and experienced clinical escorts.","ICS recommendation 55 says higher-risk patients must have two appropriately trained, competent and experienced clinical escorts, informed by the risk assessment.",
   [("Nothing, because a single nurse can cope as long as a transport assistant helps with the trolley and the doors.","A transport assistant is not a clinical escort."),("That the ambulance crew can act as the second clinical escort for the journey.","The guidance says NHS ambulance crew must not be used as the second clinical escort."),("That the doctor can follow the trolley a few minutes later in case a problem develops.","An escort has to be with the patient throughout.")],[ICS],"Rec 53-57: risk assessment; two clinical escorts for higher risk."),
 Q("communication","On arrival in the receiving unit, the transfer doctor gives a handover to the receiving doctor while you give a separate one to the receiving nurse. What does the guidance prefer?",
   "A single formal multidisciplinary handover rather than separate medical and nursing conversations.","ICS recommendation 85 says multidisciplinary handover should occur rather than separate medical and nursing conversations.",
   [("Separate handovers are best, because each professional group can then give more detail for its own role.","The guidance prefers one shared handover."),("A brief handover at the bedside is enough, since the written notes hold the detail the team needs.","The guidance calls for a formal handover as well as records."),("Handover can wait until the patient has been settled and the monitors are reconnected for an hour.","Handover should occur on arrival.")],[ICS],"Rec 84-85: formal multidisciplinary handover on arrival."),
 Q("safety","Before leaving, you notice the patient is not strapped to the trolley and a bag of drugs and consumables is resting loose between his legs. What should you do?",
   "Secure the patient with the harness or belt system and stow all portable equipment and bags securely.","ICS recommendations 77 and 79 call for patients to be secured with an appropriate harness or belt system and for portable equipment and bags to be securely stowed.",
   [("Leave things as they are, since the trolley has side rails and the corridor is short.","Side rails alone do not replace the harness or secure stowage."),("Tuck the bag under the pillow so it is within easy reach if an emergency occurs on the way.","Loose items can injure the patient or become projectiles."),("Strap the patient only when the transfer is by ambulance, because corridors are low-risk journeys.","The standards apply to intra- and inter-hospital transfers alike.")],[ICS],"Rec 77, 79: secure patient and stow equipment."),
])
S("XQ-05","chest-drain","Chest drain monitoring (BTS clinical statement 2023)",["SK-13"],[BTS],[("Asciak R et al. British Thoracic Society Clinical Statement on pleural procedures. Thorax 2023","https://brit-thoracic.org.uk/document-library/clinical-statements/pleural-procedures/bts-clinical-statement-on-pleural-procedures")],[
 Q("application","You are moving a patient with an underwater-seal chest drain bottle from bed to chair. Where should the bottle be?",
   "Below the insertion site at all times, kept upright.","The BTS statement says the drain bottle must be kept below the insertion site at all times and the drain must be kept upright.",
   [("Beside the patient on the chair, level with the drain site, so the tubing is not stretched.","A bottle at the insertion level risks fluid or air returning to the pleural space."),("On the patient's lap, tilted slightly, so it cannot be knocked over during the transfer.","A tilted bottle can break the water seal."),("Hung from the drip stand above the chair, which makes the readings easier to see.","Raising the bottle above the insertion site is unsafe.")],[BTS],"Bottle below insertion site; keep drain upright."),
 Q("judgment","A patient who had a drain placed for a large effusion develops repetitive coughing and chest pain as the fluid drains. What is the most appropriate response?",
   "Report at once, because re-expansion pulmonary oedema is a potentially fatal complication, and follow the instruction on clamping.","The BTS statement says a drain for effusion should be clamped promptly in patients with repetitive coughing or chest pain to avoid re-expansion pulmonary oedema, a potentially fatal complication.",
   [("Encourage the patient to keep coughing to clear the lungs and recheck the drainage in an hour.","The symptoms may signal re-expansion pulmonary oedema."),("Increase the drainage by lowering the bottle, so the effusion clears faster and the symptoms settle.","Faster drainage is the opposite of what the symptoms call for."),("Give the next analgesia and observe, since coughing is common while a lung re-expands.","Treating the symptom and waiting delays recognition of a dangerous complication.")],[BTS],"Clamp promptly with repetitive coughing/chest pain (RPO); controlled drainage of large effusions."),
 Q("safety","The drain bottle is bubbling and a colleague wants to clamp the tubing before taking the patient for transport. What is the best response?",
   "Do not clamp it; clamping a bubbling drain should be avoided unless under specialist pleural supervision, so seek instruction.","The BTS statement says clamping a bubbling chest tube should be avoided unless under specialist pleural supervision and in specific circumstances only.",
   [("Clamp it close to the chest wall, which is safe for a short journey and keeps the bottle level.","A clamped bubbling drain can allow air to build up in the pleural space."),("Clamp it and remove the bottle from the circuit so the patient can be moved on a clear trolley.","Disconnecting an unsupervised, clamped drain adds risk."),("Clamp it only while the lift is moving, because the motion is the main risk to the seal.","Clamping a bubbling drain is not made safe by the length of time.")],[BTS],"Clamping a bubbling chest tube should be avoided unless under specialist pleural supervision."),
 Q("documentation","What should be recorded on the dedicated chest drain observation chart?",
   "Daily wound checks, drainage volumes, and the presence of respiratory swinging and bubbling.","The BTS statement says drains should be checked daily for wound infection, and fluid drainage volumes and the presence of respiratory swinging and/or bubbling should be documented on a dedicated chest drain observation chart.",
   [("Only the colour of the fluid, because volumes and swinging are checked by the medical team.","The statement names volumes and swinging or bubbling as documentation items."),("Only the time of each bottle change, as the record of when the system was last handled.","Bottle changes alone do not show drain function."),("A single daily total for the whole day, entered at the end of the shift without observations.","Drainage should be observed and recorded over time, as instructed.")],[BTS],"Daily check; document volumes, swinging, bubbling on dedicated chart."),
 Q("scope","Shortly after a drain is inserted, a colleague asks you to connect suction 'to speed things up', but no suction has been prescribed. What should you do?",
   "Not connect it, as suction should be avoided soon after insertion and its pressure should be prescribed or documented first.","The BTS statement says suction should be avoided soon after insertion to minimise the risk of re-expansion pulmonary oedema, and pressures should be prescribed or documented by the medical team before starting.",
   [("Connect it at a low setting, because gentle suction is unlikely to cause any harm to the lung.","Even low suction soon after insertion carries the re-expansion risk, and it needs a prescribed pressure."),("Connect it and document the setting afterwards, since the medical team can review it later.","Pressures should be prescribed before suction starts."),("Connect it only for patients who are breathless, to make drainage quicker and relieve symptoms.","Breathlessness does not remove the need for a prescription.")],[BTS],"Suction avoided soon after insertion; pressures prescribed/documented first."),
 Q("monitoring","At the start of the shift the drain bottle has very little water in it. What is the correct nursing action?",
   "Treat it as a system fault: the system needs enough water to cover the end of the tube.","The BTS statement says the drain must have adequate water in the system to cover the end of the tube.",
   [("Ignore it, because the water level only matters while the patient is breathing out strongly.","The water seal matters whenever the drain is in use."),("Top it up with any available sterile water without checking the system, as the level looks low.","Handling the system follows an aseptic procedure and local instruction."),("Raise the bottle above the patient so the little water left covers the tube end.","Raising the bottle is unsafe; the bottle stays below the insertion site.")],[BTS],"Adequate water in system to cover end of tube; ANTT when changing bottle/tubing."),
])
S("XQ-06","mobilization","Safe mobilization of ventilated patients (Hodgson 2014)",["SK-16"],[HOD],[("Hodgson CL et al. Expert consensus and recommendations on safety criteria for active mobilization of mechanically ventilated critically ill adults. Crit Care 2014;18:658","https://doi.org/10.1186/s13054-014-0658-y")],[
 Q("judgment","The physiotherapist asks whether an intubated patient can sit on the edge of the bed. A colleague says the endotracheal tube rules it out. What does the consensus say?",
   "An endotracheal tube is not in itself a contraindication to early mobilization.","The 2014 consensus states that intubation via an endotracheal tube was not a contraindication to early mobilization.",
   [("The colleague is right: any patient with an endotracheal tube should stay in bed until extubated.","The consensus says the opposite."),("Mobilization is only allowed after tracheostomy, because the tube is then more secure.","The consensus does not require a tracheostomy."),("Mobilization is allowed only in bed, never out of bed, while a tube is in place.","The respiratory criteria apply to in- and out-of-bed mobilization.")],[HOD],"Intubation via ETT not a contraindication to early mobilization."),
 Q("safety","Before each mobilization session for a ventilated patient, which checks does the consensus describe?",
   "That any artificial airway is correctly positioned and secure, and that oxygen is available with a reserve longer than the expected activity.","The consensus says an appropriate professional should check before each episode that any artificial airway is correctly positioned and secure and that supplemental oxygen has a reserve exceeding the expected duration.",
   [("That the patient has had a full meal, so they have the energy for the activity.","This is not a safety check described in the consensus."),("That the family is present, so they can reassure the patient during the activity.","Family presence is not a safety criterion."),("That the sedation has been completely stopped for at least a full day beforehand.","This is not a criterion the consensus describes.")],[HOD],"Check airway position/security and oxygen reserve before each episode."),
 Q("judgment","A patient's oxygen saturation, FiO2 and PEEP are all at the edge of the unit's safety limits at once, and the team wants to sit him out. What does the consensus advise?",
   "Consult an experienced medical team before mobilizing.","The consensus says that if the patient is at the safety limits for several categories (for example low saturation, high FiO2 and high PEEP), an experienced medical team should be consulted before mobilization.",
   [("Go ahead, because each value is still within its own limit when looked at one by one.","The consensus asks for a senior discussion when several limits are reached together."),("Go ahead and mobilize more slowly than usual so the patient has time to adapt to the change.","Slowing down is not the advice given."),("Cancel all mobilization for the rest of the admission, since the limits were reached once.","The advice is to consult, not to cancel permanently.")],[HOD],"At limits in several categories: consult experienced medical team."),
 Q("scope","A ventilated patient on a vasoactive infusion has had his dose increased twice this morning. A colleague says any vasoactive drug means no mobilization. What is the most accurate view?",
   "Vasoactive drugs are not an absolute contraindication, but rising doses call for caution or contraindicate mobilization, and the unit decides case by case.","The consensus found vasoactive drugs per se were not an absolute contraindication; appropriateness depends on the dose, the change in dose (rising doses call for caution or contraindication) and perfusion, and units agree thresholds case by case.",
   [("The colleague is right: any vasoactive drug means mobilization is not allowed.","The consensus does not say this."),("A single threshold dose is agreed worldwide and the nurse can look it up in the paper.","The panel could not agree a threshold dose."),("Rising doses are irrelevant as long as the blood pressure reading on the monitor looks acceptable.","The change in dose and perfusion are both part of the consideration.")],[HOD],"Vasoactive drugs not absolute contraindication; dose, change in dose and perfusion matter; no consensus on a threshold."),
 Q("application","A patient has an oxygen saturation of 94%, FiO2 of 0.45 and a respiratory rate of 22 and no other contraindication. How do these respiratory values compare with the consensus criteria?",
   "They meet the criteria described as safe for in- and out-of-bed mobilization.","The consensus considered FiO2 below 0.6, saturation above 90% and respiratory rate below 30 breaths per minute safe criteria for in- and out-of-bed mobilization if there are no other contraindications.",
   [("They do not meet the criteria, because the FiO2 must be at room air before any mobilization.","The criterion is FiO2 below 0.6, not room air."),("They meet the criteria only for in-bed exercises, never for sitting out of bed.","The criteria apply to in- and out-of-bed mobilization."),("They are irrelevant, as the consensus gives no respiratory values for safe mobilization.","The consensus gives those values.")],[HOD],"FiO2 <0.6, SpO2 >90%, RR <30 considered safe criteria if no other contraindications."),
 Q("scope","A nurse asks whether the consensus criteria mean she can mobilize ventilated patients without the team's judgement. What is the best answer?",
   "No: the criteria are a guide to be used with clinical judgement, and each unit should consider them against its own staffing and expertise.","The authors state the criteria should be regarded as a guide and used with clinical judgement, are appropriate for experienced ICU clinicians, and each ICU should consider them in light of its own staffing levels and expertise.",
   [("Yes, once the numbers are met she may mobilize alone without any team agreement.","The consensus does not remove the need for judgement and team input."),("Yes, because the criteria were issued by a regulator and replace local policy.","They are expert consensus, not a regulatory standard."),("No, because the criteria apply only to patients who are not receiving any oxygen.","The criteria were written for ventilated patients.")],[HOD],"Criteria a guide; use with clinical judgement; consider local staffing and expertise."),
])
S("XQ-07","crrt-alarms","CRRT alarm response and prescription checks",["SK-17"],[AHS,"S-KDIGO-AKI-2012"],[("Alberta Health Services. CRRT Alarm Management Care Pathway Resource (January 2025)","https://www.albertahealthservices.ca/assets/about/scn/ahs-scn-cc-dialyzingwisely-crrt-alarm-management-care-pathway-resource.pdf"),("KDIGO Clinical Practice Guideline for Acute Kidney Injury (2012)","https://kdigo.org/guidelines/acute-kidney-injury/")],[
 Q("judgment","An access-pressure alarm sounds repeatedly on a patient's CRRT machine. Which first step fits the care pathway?",
   "Check the patient, then the lines, clamps and catheter position, before clearing the alarm.","The pathway says to check the patient, review and address the alarm on the screen before clearing it, and for access and return alarms to check the patient, clamps or kinks, and the dialysis catheter.",
   [("Press the override button and carry on, because repeated alarms are usually false alarms.","Override and disable buttons decrease alarm sensitivity and are not a fix."),("Stop the blood pump and disconnect the patient straight away, without looking for a cause.","Return and disconnection follow if the alarm cannot be managed, not as a first step."),("Increase the blood flow rate as far as the machine allows to overcome the alarm.","The pathway says to adjust the blood flow rate, not to push it to the maximum.")],[AHS],"Check patient first; review alarm before clearing; access/return alarm checks."),
 Q("safety","A colleague silences a recurring CRRT alarm using the disable function to 'keep the room quiet'. What is the concern?",
   "Alarm off, disable and override buttons decrease alarm sensitivity.","The pathway states that alarm off, disable and override buttons decrease alarm sensitivity and that the alarm should be reviewed and addressed before clearing.",
   [("There is no concern, since the machine will still protect the patient from every fault.","Disabling alarms reduces that protection."),("The only concern is noise for other patients, which a quieter setting would fix.","The concern is safety, not noise."),("The concern is minor, because the manufacturer's support line can always reset the alarm remotely.","A remote reset does not replace addressing the cause.")],[AHS],"Alarm off/disable/override decrease alarm sensitivity."),
 Q("escalation","You have tried the usual checks but the alarms cannot be resolved and treatment keeps stopping. What does the pathway suggest?",
   "Call manufacturer support, return blood if possible, document why, and tell the physician.","The pathway says that if alarms cannot be managed, call customer support, return blood when possible and end treatment, document the reason for stopping, and notify the physician, who may need to rewire or insert a new line.",
   [("Keep restarting the treatment until it runs, and tell the doctor only at the end of the shift when handing over.","Repeated restarts waste treatment time and delay escalation."),("Leave the blood in the circuit and clamp the lines until the next shift can look at it.","Blood should be returned when possible, not left in a stopped circuit."),("Disconnect the patient and discard the circuit without recording why treatment was stopped.","The reason for stopping must be documented.")],[AHS],"If alarms cannot be managed: support, return blood, document, notify MD."),
 Q("application","A bag-weight alarm sounds. According to the pathway, which check is appropriate?",
   "Look for bags touching other objects, leaking or sitting on the wrong scale, and check connections.","The pathway lists, for flow alarms, checking whether the line is clamped or kinked and whether bags are touching other objects altering weights, leaking, or on the wrong scale.",
   [("Replace every bag on the machine at the same time to reset all of the scales together.","The pathway advises changing one bag at a time and closing each scale."),("Remove the patient from the machine and reconnect once all bags have been weighed by hand.","Disconnecting is not the first step for a weight alarm."),("Restart the machine in a different mode so the weight reading no longer matters.","Changing modes without a cause is not advised.")],[AHS],"Flow alarms: check clamps/kinks, bags touching objects, leaking, wrong scale."),
 Q("monitoring","A patient's CRRT is running but the delivered dose is lower than prescribed because of repeated interruptions. What does KDIGO support?",
   "Reporting the under-delivery, because frequent assessment of the delivered dose is recommended.","The KDIGO AKI guideline recommends frequent assessment of the delivered dose of renal replacement therapy to ensure the prescribed dose is delivered.",
   [("Ignoring it, because the prescription is a target and not a measure of what is delivered.","Delivered dose is exactly what the guideline asks to be assessed."),("Increasing the dose yourself to make up the difference without an order.","Changing the prescription is a prescriber decision."),("Recording it only in the nursing notes so that the next shift can decide what to do.","Under-delivery should be reported so the team can respond.")],["S-KDIGO-AKI-2012"],"Frequent assessment of delivered RRT dose."),
 Q("safety","A patient on CRRT with heparin-containing flushes is suspected of heparin-induced thrombocytopenia. What does the guideline-based draft say the nurse must do?",
   "Tell the prescriber now and make sure all heparin, including flushes, is stopped.","KDIGO advises that if HIT is suspected or confirmed, all heparin must be stopped, including in flushes and locks; the nurse informs the prescriber immediately and follows local policy on the extent.",
   [("Continue the heparin flushes because they contain only a small amount of the drug.","Flush heparin is a source of exposure and local policy decides the extent."),("Switch to a different heparin brand and monitor, since the brand may be the cause.","All heparin must stop, not just one brand."),("Wait for the platelet count to fall further before telling anyone, to be sure of the diagnosis.","Suspected HIT is reported immediately.")],["S-KDIGO-AKI-2012"],"Suspected/confirmed HIT: stop all heparin including flushes and locks (extent per local policy)."),
])
S("XQ-08","vasoactive-safety","Vasoactive medication safety (FDA labels)",["MD-01","MD-03","MD-04","MD-05","MD-02"],["L-NOREPINEPHRINE","L-EPINEPHRINE","L-VASOPRESSIN","L-DOBUTAMINE","L-MILRINONE"],[("DailyMed labels as listed in content/drafts/medications/LABEL_SOURCES.csv","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/")],[
 Q("safety","A patient's norepinephrine is infusing through a peripheral line when the skin over the cannula becomes pale, cool and swollen. What should you do?",
   "Treat it as possible extravasation: stop the infusion per protocol, report at once and follow the prescriber's instructions.","The label warns that extravasation into tissue can cause local necrosis through vasoconstriction; management, including any infiltration treatment, is a prescriber or protocol decision.",
   [("Slow the infusion and recheck in an hour, because the change may be a reaction to the cold drug.","Delay risks tissue necrosis."),("Massage the area to disperse the drug, since this spreads it over a wider area and reduces harm.","Massage is not advised and may worsen tissue injury."),("Apply a cold pack for comfort and continue the infusion until the next scheduled site check.","Continuing an infusion through a suspected extravasation worsens the injury.")],["L-NOREPINEPHRINE"],"Label: tissue ischaemia; avoid extravasation; local necrosis."),
 Q("judgment","The line carrying a patient's norepinephrine becomes occluded and the pump alarms. The patient's blood pressure is falling. What is the best action?",
   "Restore the infusion urgently and escalate.","The label warns that sudden cessation of the infusion may result in marked hypotension.",
   [("Wait for the pump to clear itself, because the alarm will stop once the pressure equalises.","An occluded line will not clear by waiting, and the interruption is dangerous."),("Disconnect the infusion and flush the line hard to remove the blockage.","A forceful flush may deliver a bolus and is not an approved response."),("Give a fluid bolus yourself as soon as the alarm sounds and then deal with the line.","Fluid is a prescriber or protocol decision; the interruption needs urgent action and escalation.")],["L-NOREPINEPHRINE"],"Label: hypotension after abrupt discontinuation."),
 Q("monitoring","Twelve hours after vasopressin is stopped, a patient's urine output rises sharply and the urine looks pale and watery, and the sodium is climbing. What should you suspect and do?",
   "Report possible reversible diabetes insipidus and monitor urine output, fluid status and electrolytes as the label advises.","The label describes reversible diabetes insipidus (polyuria, dilute urine, hypernatraemia) after stopping vasopressin and advises monitoring serum electrolytes, fluid status and urine output.",
   [("Reassure the team that this is the diuretic phase of recovery and record the output only.","The label names a specific cause that needs reporting."),("Restart the vasopressin yourself to see whether the urine output settles.","Restarting is a prescriber decision."),("Restrict all fluids until the morning blood tests, to protect the sodium level.","Fluid management is a prescriber decision.")],["L-VASOPRESSIN"],"Label: reversible diabetes insipidus after cessation; monitor electrolytes, fluid status, urine output."),
 Q("monitoring","A patient with atrial fibrillation on dobutamine has a sudden rise in heart rate to a rapid, irregular rhythm. What does the label's warning suggest?",
   "Report it promptly as a possible rapid ventricular response.","The label warns that because dobutamine facilitates atrioventricular conduction, patients with atrial fibrillation are at risk of a rapid ventricular response.",
   [("Treat it as a normal effect of the drug and record the rate at the next hourly check.","A marked rise needs prompt reporting."),("Stop the infusion permanently and record that the patient is intolerant of the drug.","Changing the infusion is a prescriber or protocol decision."),("Give extra fluid yourself, because a fast heart rate always means the patient is dry.","Fluid is a prescriber decision and the cause here may be the drug.")],["L-DOBUTAMINE"],"Label: AF patients at risk of rapid ventricular response."),
 Q("monitoring","A patient on milrinone has more ventricular ectopic beats appearing on the monitor over an hour. What does the label support?",
   "Report promptly, because the label links milrinone with more ventricular arrhythmias and calls for continuous ECG monitoring.","The label says milrinone has been associated with increased ventricular arrhythmias, including non-sustained ventricular tachycardia, and that patients should be observed closely with continuous electrocardiographic monitoring.",
   [("Silence the alarm limits so the ectopics stop triggering the monitor and wait for the ward round.","Widening alarm limits hides the problem."),("Do nothing because ectopic beats are harmless in patients with heart failure.","The label treats ventricular arrhythmia as an important risk."),("Stop the continuous ECG because it is causing the alarms, and take a pulse each hour instead.","Continuous ECG monitoring is the label's expectation.")],["L-MILRINONE"],"Label: ventricular arrhythmias; continuous ECG monitoring."),
 Q("application","You are asked to give epinephrine by injection for anaphylaxis. Which site does the label identify as the most appropriate?",
   "The anterolateral thigh, avoiding the buttocks, digits, hands and feet.","The label says injection into the anterolateral thigh (vastus lateralis) is the most appropriate site and that epinephrine should not be injected into the buttocks, digits, hands or feet.",
   [("The deltoid, because the arm is the quickest site to reach in a patient who is lying down.","The label says injection into smaller muscles such as the deltoid is not recommended."),("The buttock, because the muscle is large and has a good blood supply.","The label says not to inject into the buttocks."),("The fingertip, which gives the fastest absorption into the bloodstream in an emergency.","The label says not to inject into digits.")],["L-EPINEPHRINE"],"Label: anterolateral thigh; not buttocks, digits, hands or feet."),
])
S("XQ-09","sedative-opioid-safety","Sedative and opioid medication safety (FDA labels)",["MD-06","MD-07","MD-08","MD-09","MD-10"],["L-PROPOFOL","L-MIDAZOLAM","L-DEXMEDETOMIDINE","L-FENTANYL","L-MORPHINE"],[("DailyMed labels as listed in content/drafts/medications/LABEL_SOURCES.csv","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/")],[
 Q("safety","Before giving intravenous midazolam to a patient for a procedure on the unit, what must be in place according to the label's boxed warning?",
   "Continuous respiratory and cardiac monitoring, resuscitation equipment on hand, and skilled airway staff.","The boxed warning says IV midazolam should be used only where continuous monitoring of respiratory and cardiac function is provided, with immediately available resuscitative drugs, equipment for bag-valve-mask ventilation and intubation, and personnel skilled in airway management.",
   [("A signed consent form and a quiet room, because the drug is routine and low risk.","Consent and a quiet room are not the safety measures the warning names."),("A nurse checking the patient's colour every fifteen minutes during the whole recovery period.","Intermittent checks are not continuous monitoring."),("The patient's family at the bedside to watch for changes in alertness while the nurse is busy.","Family cannot replace trained monitoring.")],["L-MIDAZOLAM"],"Boxed warning: respiratory depression; continuous monitoring; resuscitation equipment; skilled personnel."),
 Q("judgment","A patient already receiving an opioid infusion is prescribed a benzodiazepine as well. What should the nurse understand about this combination?",
   "It can cause profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma and death.","The opioid boxed warnings say concomitant use with benzodiazepines or other CNS depressants may cause profound sedation, respiratory depression, coma and death.",
   [("It is a routine combination with no added risk, so standard observations are enough.","The labels warn of a specific added risk."),("It is safe if the two drugs are given at least ten minutes apart in the same patient.","The risk concerns concomitant use, not the gap between doses."),("It matters only in patients who have never had an opioid before this admission.","The warning applies broadly.")],["L-FENTANYL","L-MORPHINE","L-MIDAZOLAM"],"Boxed warnings: concomitant opioids/benzodiazepines/CNS depressants."),
 Q("safety","A nurse is about to draw a second dose from a propofol vial that was opened for a previous patient 'because there is plenty left'. What is the correct response?",
   "Do not use it: the label says vials are never accessed more than once or used on more than one person, and unused product is discarded.","The label states strict aseptic technique must be maintained, vials are never to be accessed more than once or used on more than one person, and unused drug product should be discarded.",
   [("It is acceptable if the vial has been kept in the fridge and the stopper wiped with alcohol.","The label does not allow reuse for another patient under any storage."),("It is acceptable for the same ward and the same day, because the emulsion stays sterile for a full shift.","The label sets time limits and forbids use on more than one person."),("It is acceptable if a new needle is used, since the needle is the main source of contamination.","A new needle does not remove the contamination risk of a reused vial.")],["L-PROPOFOL"],"Label: strict aseptic technique; vials never accessed more than once or used on more than one person; discard unused."),
 Q("monitoring","A patient sedated with dexmedetomidine has a heart rate that falls and a blood pressure that drops over twenty minutes. What does the label support?",
   "Report promptly, since bradycardia and hypotension may need medical intervention.","The label warns of hypotension, bradycardia and sinus arrest, which may need medical intervention, and says patients should be continuously monitored.",
   [("Treat it as the intended sedative effect and record the readings at the next routine check.","The label asks for reporting, not acceptance of falling vital signs."),("Increase the drug to deepen sedation so the patient settles and the readings steady.","Dose changes are a prescriber or protocol decision, and the readings suggest a problem."),("Stop monitoring the heart rhythm to avoid false alarms while the patient is sleeping.","Continuous monitoring is required.")],["L-DEXMEDETOMIDINE"],"Label: continuous monitoring; hypotension, bradycardia and sinus arrest."),
 Q("application","An elderly, frail patient with chronic lung disease has just received a morphine dose. Which preparation does the label support?",
   "Close respiratory monitoring, and naloxone and resuscitation equipment immediately available.","The label warns of life-threatening respiratory depression in patients with chronic pulmonary disease or the elderly, cachectic or debilitated and advises close monitoring, and has naloxone and resuscitative equipment immediately available where cardiovascular instability is a risk.",
   [("Routine observations once a shift, because morphine is a very familiar drug on the unit.","Familiarity does not lower the risk in this patient."),("Keep the patient flat and covered so they sleep more soundly after the dose.","Sleeping soundly is a warning sign, not a goal."),("Give a second sedative with it to prevent any restlessness after the pain has gone.","Adding a CNS depressant raises the risk.")],["L-MORPHINE"],"Label: close monitoring in chronic pulmonary disease/elderly/debilitated; naloxone and resuscitative equipment available."),
 Q("judgment","Shortly after a rapid fentanyl dose, a patient's chest becomes stiff and ventilation is difficult. What is the correct first response?",
   "Call for help immediately; the label warns of muscle rigidity that the prescriber manages.","The label lists the risk of skeletal muscle rigidity and says it is managed with a neuromuscular blocking agent, which is a prescriber decision.",
   [("Reassure the patient and wait for the stiffness to wear off before doing anything further.","Rigidity that limits ventilation is an emergency."),("Give a second opioid dose to relieve the discomfort and relax the chest muscles.","Another opioid dose does not treat rigidity and may worsen it."),("Remove the pulse oximeter so the alarm stops while the team is called.","Monitoring is essential during the event.")],["L-FENTANYL"],"Label: skeletal muscle rigidity; managed with neuromuscular blocking agent."),
])
S("XQ-10","enteral-tube-position","Nasogastric tube position and feeding safety (NICE CG32, NHS alert)",["SK-10","SK-11"],["S-NICE-CG32","S-NHSE-NGT-2016"],[("NICE CG32 Nutrition support for adults","https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/cg32"),("NHS Improvement Patient Safety Alert: Nasogastric tube misplacement (2016)","https://www.england.nhs.uk/")],[
 Q("safety","You are about to start a feed through a nasogastric tube that has been in for two days. What must be done first according to NICE?",
   "Confirm the tube position before the feed, using aspiration and pH-graded paper, with X-ray if necessary.","NICE CG32 says the position of nasogastric tubes should be confirmed after placement and before each use by aspiration and pH-graded paper, with X-ray if necessary.",
   [("Nothing extra, because the position was confirmed when the tube was passed two days ago.","Position is checked before each use."),("Listen over the stomach while injecting air, since the sound shows the tube is in the stomach.","This is not an accepted way to confirm position."),("Check that the mark at the nostril has not moved, then start the feed without testing aspirate.","A marker check supplements but does not replace confirmation as the protocol requires.")],["S-NICE-CG32"],"Confirm position before each use by aspiration and pH-graded paper (X-ray if necessary)."),
 Q("judgment","You cannot aspirate any fluid from a patient's nasogastric tube before the scheduled feed. What is the safest approach?",
   "Do not feed; follow the failed-aspiration protocol and escalate until position is confirmed.","NICE says local protocols should address how to proceed when aspiration is not possible; the NHS alert stresses that using a misplaced tube can cause severe harm.",
   [("Start the feed slowly and watch the patient closely for coughing, since the tube position was confirmed earlier in the admission.","A previous check does not make this use safe."),("Flush a large volume of water to check that it goes in easily and then start the feed if there is no resistance.","Flushing an unconfirmed tube is unsafe."),("Reposition the tube a few centimetres and begin feeding without any further check.","Moving the tube without confirming position adds risk.")],["S-NICE-CG32","S-NHSE-NGT-2016"],"Local protocol for failed aspiration; position must be confirmed before use."),
 Q("monitoring","A patient on a proton pump inhibitor has aspirate with a pH above the usual cut-off. How should the nurse interpret this?",
   "Recognise that acid suppression can invalidate the pH check and escalate before using the tube.","NICE says local protocols should address how to proceed when the pH check is invalid because of gastric acid suppression.",
   [("Accept the result as proof that the tube is in the lung and remove it at once.","A high pH may be due to acid suppression, not lung placement; the protocol guides the next step."),("Accept the result as confirming stomach placement because higher values are safer.","A high pH is not confirmation."),("Repeat the test on every shift until a low pH is obtained, and feed in the meantime.","Feeding must wait for a valid confirmation.")],["S-NICE-CG32"],"Protocol for invalid pH due to gastric acid suppression."),
 Q("assessment","Which daily checks of a nasogastric tube does NICE list?",
   "Fixation, nasal erosion, and whether the tube is intact, unblocked and unkinked.","NICE CG32 lists daily checks of fixation, nasal erosion and whether the tube is intact, unblocked and unkinked.",
   [("Only the colour of the tube, since discolouration shows it needs changing.","Colour is not the listed check."),("The patient's weight and height, because these show whether the tube is the right size.","These are nutrition measures, not tube checks."),("Whether the feed bag label has been signed, as the main safety check on the tube.","Labels matter but are not the daily tube checks listed.")],["S-NICE-CG32"],"Daily check of fixation, nasal erosion, tube intact/unblocked/unkinked."),
 Q("scope","What does the NHS 2016 alert say about introducing fluid or medicine into the lungs through a misplaced nasogastric tube?",
   "It is a Never Event in England, which is why checking placement before use is essential.","The 2016 NHS alert states that introducing fluids or medication into the respiratory tract or pleura through a misplaced nasogastric tube is a Never Event and that checks before use are essential.",
   [("It is an expected complication for which no special check is needed.","The alert treats it as a preventable Never Event."),("It is rare enough that an annual audit is the only safeguard required.","The alert requires checking before each use."),("It matters only for children, because adult tubes rarely enter the airway.","The alert concerns misplaced tubes in general.")],["S-NHSE-NGT-2016"],"Never Event: fluid or medication via misplaced NG tube into respiratory tract/pleura."),
 Q("scope","Who should insert and confirm the position of nasogastric tubes, according to NICE?",
   "Staff with the relevant skills and training.","NICE CG32 says nasogastric tubes should be inserted by staff with the relevant skills and training.",
   [("Any staff member on the shift, because tube passing is a basic nursing task everyone has done.","NICE requires relevant skills and training."),("Only the on-call doctor, because nurses are not permitted to pass tubes under any circumstances.","NICE does not limit it to doctors; it requires trained staff."),("The patient's relatives, after being shown once how to pass the tube.","Insertion is for trained staff.")],["S-NICE-CG32"],"Tubes inserted by staff with relevant skills and training."),
])
S("XQ-11","scope-escalation","Scope of practice and escalation across the skills library",["SK-01","SK-13","SK-16","SK-17","SK-20"],[BSI,BTS,HOD,AHS,ICS,"S-KDIGO-AKI-2012"],[("Sources as listed in the skill drafts","https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/")],[
 Q("scope","A patient's chest drain is bubbling and the nurse thinks suction might help. Who decides on suction and its pressure?",
   "The medical or specialist team, who prescribe or document the suction pressure before it starts.","The BTS statement says suction pressures should be prescribed or documented by the medical team before suction is commenced.",
   [("The nurse, because the nurse is the one who observes the drain most often.","Observation does not make suction a nursing decision."),("The bedside nurse and the patient together, once the patient says it is comfortable.","Comfort does not set suction pressure."),("The healthcare assistant who connects the equipment.","Support staff do not set clinical pressures.")],[BTS],"Suction pressures prescribed or documented by the medical team."),
 Q("scope","The arterial line on a stable patient has not been used for sampling for a day. Who decides to remove it?",
   "The prescriber; the nurse asks each day whether the line is still needed.","The arterial line draft rests on CDC advice to remove catheters as soon as they are no longer needed; removal is a prescriber decision and the nurse raises the daily question.",
   [("The nurse alone, once the line has been unused for a full day.","Removal needs an order or approved protocol."),("The patient, once he asks for it to come out.","Patient preference is considered but does not replace the clinical decision."),("The pharmacist, who reviews the daily infusion chart.","Line removal is not a pharmacy decision.")],[BSI],"Remove arterial catheter as soon as no longer needed; prescriber decision."),
 Q("scope","A CRRT prescription says one fluid-balance goal, but the patient seems wetter than planned. Who changes the prescription?",
   "The prescriber; the nurse reports the discrepancy and the delivered dose or balance.","KDIGO recommends frequent assessment of delivered dose, and prescription decisions rest with the prescribing team; the nurse reports.",
   [("The nurse, who adjusts the goal on the screen to match what the machine is actually delivering.","Changing the prescription is not a nursing decision."),("The machine, which adjusts itself automatically to correct any imbalance.","The machine does not change the prescription."),("The next shift, who will notice any change when they take over.","Delaying the report delays correction.")],["S-KDIGO-AKI-2012"],"Frequent assessment of delivered dose; prescription by prescriber."),
 Q("scope","A nurse wonders whether she can increase a patient's vasoactive infusion to allow him to sit out for physiotherapy. What is the best answer?",
   "No; dose changes are for the prescriber or an approved protocol, case by case.","The mobilization consensus found no agreed vasoactive dose threshold and said units decide case by case; dose changes remain a prescriber or protocol decision.",
   [("Yes, because she knows the patient best and the change is small and temporary.","Size and duration do not remove the need for an order."),("Yes, if the physiotherapist agrees, since the two professionals share the responsibility.","Physiotherapist agreement is not a prescription."),("No, because vasoactive drugs always rule out sitting out, whatever the dose.","The consensus says they are not an absolute contraindication.")],[HOD],"Vasoactive dose threshold decided by the unit case by case; no consensus."),
 Q("scope","Who makes the decision to transfer a critically ill patient to another department or hospital, according to the transfer guidance?",
   "The appropriate consultants, not the escorting nurse.","ICS recommendation 42 says the decision to transfer and to accept a patient should be made by appropriate consultants.",
   [("The escorting nurse, who knows the equipment and the route best.","The decision rests with consultants."),("The receiving ward manager, once a bed becomes available.","Bed availability does not make the clinical decision."),("The transport assistant, who is booked to take the patient at the time requested.","The transport assistant does not make clinical decisions.")],[ICS],"Rec 42: decision to transfer and accept made by appropriate consultants."),
 Q("escalation","A patient on a vasoactive infusion has a cold, pale, painful arm with an arterial line. What is the nurse's role?",
   "Report at once and follow instructions, keeping the arm visible.","The arterial line draft lists cold, pale or painful limb as an escalation trigger, with decisions about replacing or removing the catheter belonging to the prescriber.",
   [("Remove the arterial line immediately without telling anyone, to restore circulation.","Removal without an order is outside the nurse's role and may cause bleeding."),("Flush the line repeatedly until the colour returns to the hand.","Repeated flushing can cause harm and does not address the cause."),("Wait and document the finding, because circulation often improves within a few hours.","Signs of limb ischaemia need immediate reporting.")],[BSI],"Cold, pale, painful limb with arterial line: escalate immediately."),
])
